//
// PROBE: RFC-0009 criterion 4 — `.id` and `.timestamp` answer on an object.
//
// These are methods on the base `Object` class
// (reference/Bund/src/stdlib/functions/oop/base_classes.rs:91-92), and `.id`
// returns a *string* (`:16-17`). ERRATA records them as the constraint that
// stopped RFC-0001 shrinking the value by dropping identity: they are public
// API, so identity had to stay answerable. RFC-0001 resolved it by moving
// identity into the heap header rather than off the value.
//
// So this probe is where D1's laziness ends for an object, and where D2's does.
// Both are reached by dispatch walking `.super` two levels — a user class does
// not carry `.id`; `Object` does.
//
// **What a probe can and cannot pin here.** An identity is a fresh nanoid per
// value, so *no* run agrees with another on it. A first draft printed the id
// and `cargo xtask golden` refused the capture — "two runs disagree after
// normalisation" — which is the harness being right: a golden holding a random
// string fails forever and teaches the reader to ignore failures.
//
// So everything below is a *predicate* over the identity, never the identity.
// Two of criterion 4's clauses cannot be reduced to a predicate at all and are
// pinned in Rust instead:
//
//   - "a **21-character** string" — the oracle registers no length word (445
//     word names across reference/Bund/src/stdlib/, none of them `len`), so
//     the length is not expressible in Bund. Pinned by
//     `crates/bund2-stdlib/src/oop.rs`'s `dispatch_reaches_an_inherited_method`.
//   - "**neither materialises** anything on a value that is never asked" — the
//     unminted state is by definition unobservable from the language. Pinned by
//     `crates/bund2-value/src/lib.rs`'s `identity_is_not_minted_until_needed`
//     and `stamps_order_by_observation_not_construction`.
//
// The class names itself per D25, which is also what lets `<class> !` build it.
//
:Probed class
  :.class_name "Probed" set
  ".super" [ :Object ] set
register

//
// `.id` and `.timestamp` **peek**: the receiver stays on the stack and the
// answer is pushed beside it (base_classes.rs:12-17). So each call leaves
// [object, answer], and `swap drop` is what keeps the object from being
// compared against the answer — which is what a first draft of this probe did.
//
// Each clause also drops its own leavings. The capture pins the final stack,
// so an id left behind would make the *stack* unreproducible even when every
// printed line agrees.
//
"probe: .id answers a String" println
:.id :Probed object ! swap drop
"String" ?type println drop

"probe: two instances of one class have distinct identities" println
:.id :Probed object ! swap drop
:.id :Probed object ! swap drop
!= println

"probe: .timestamp answers a non-zero Float" println
:.timestamp :Probed object ! swap drop
"Float" ?type println drop
:.timestamp :Probed object ! swap drop
0.0 != println

debug.display_stack
